  src_login: "Login", src_user: "Username", src_pass: "Password", src_pass_saved: "•••••• saved", src_show: "Show password", src_silent: "connected, but only silence: microphone muted or blocked? (phone: another app uses the microphone, audio off in the app, permission missing)", dev_discard: "Discard unsaved changes?",
  s_dev: "Settings for", f_dev: "Device", dev_all: "All devices (default)", dev_gen: "All devices", st_dev1: "Device", h_dev: "These values apply to all devices. Pick a device above to see only its settings or set its own. Gain, name and login are in the device card in the “Device” tab.", f_devown: "Own settings for this device", h_devown_off: "Off: this device uses the general values (grey). Switch on to set threshold, minimum duration, pre/post-roll and recording hours for this device only.", h_devown_on: "On: the values below apply to this device only.", own_tag: "own settings", own_short: "own", dev_settings: "Level & threshold", saved_dev: "Saved for {n}. Applies immediately.",
  s_sched: "Different values at certain times", h_sched: "For example more sensitive at night than during the day. Outside these times the values above apply. If two overlap, the upper one wins.", slot_add: "Add time profile", slot_now: "active now", slot_tag: "time profile {s}", from: "from", to: "to",
  t_device: "Which microphone on the Home Assistant server is used. “Default” is the input chosen in Home Assistant under Settings → System → Hardware → Audio.", t_mic_volume: "How strongly the server microphone records. If the level in complete silence is below −85 dB, more sensitivity helps. Above 100 % is digital gain (a bit more noise).", t_sample_rate: "How finely the sound is sampled. 16000 Hz is enough for knocking, banging and voices and saves space. 44100/48000 = CD quality, larger files.", t_thr_mode: "The threshold is the loudness from which a disturbance is counted.\n• Fixed: always the same value.\n• Adapts: always a bit above the normal background (fridge, street).\n• Adapts, with limits: like “adapts”, but never below the lower limit and never above the upper limit.", t_thr_fixed_db: "Any sound louder than this counts. Values are negative: −80 is very quiet, −20 very loud. Tip: read in the level bar above what a knock reaches and set a bit below.", t_offset_db: "How much louder than the normal background a sound must be to count. 6 dB ≈ twice as loud. Too small (1–3) = normal noise triggers all the time. Too large (over 15) = quiet knocks are missed.", t_min_db: "In complete silence the threshold would sink very low and every tiny sound would trigger. It never goes below this. Example: −75 = sounds quieter than −75 dB never count.", t_thr_max_db: "When it is loud for a long time (e.g. TV), the threshold rises. It never goes above this, so a knock is still detected.", t_bg_window: "How far back the program looks to learn what “normally quiet” is. Short (e.g. 30 s) = adapts quickly. Long (e.g. 10 min) = calmer, short sounds change nothing.", t_bg_stat: "• Quietest moments: takes the quietest 10 % of the time. Knocks and short sounds do not distort the background.\n• Typical level: the median. Reacts more to longer sounds.\n• Quietest value: the lowest level in the period. A single very quiet moment lowers the threshold.\n• Average: the average of all levels in the period. Longer and louder sounds raise it more than the typical level.\n• Loudest value: the highest level in the period. The threshold is then above the loudest sound of the recent past – only what is louder than everything before is detected.", t_rise_s: "How fast the threshold rises when it stays louder. 300 s = very slow: repeated knocking does not raise the threshold. 30 s = fast: e.g. a fan switched on is quickly learned as “normal”.", t_fall_s: "How fast the threshold falls when it gets quieter (e.g. at night). Small (10 s) = quickly sensitive again.", t_min_event_ms: "How long a sound must stay above the threshold to count. 0 = even a single short knock. 40–60 ms filters out electrical clicks, knocks are still detected. Measured in 20 ms steps.", t_pre_roll_s: "The sound of the last seconds is held briefly in memory only. When a disturbance comes, the detection also analyses its beginning. Sound is never stored, only measurements.", t_post_roll_s: "After the last sound the event continues this long. A new knock in this time belongs to the same event. Example: 5 s = knocking with pauses under 5 s becomes one event.", t_max_clip_s: "With long noise a new event is started after this time so entries stay clear. Nothing is lost.",  t_auto_delete: "If on, noise tracks, videos and entries are deleted after the set number of days. For evidence, better leave it off.", t_retention_days: "How many days recordings are kept when automatic deletion is on.", t_active_from: "Disturbances are only counted and recorded in this time. Both fields empty = always. Across midnight works too, e.g. 22:00 to 07:00.", t_active_to: "End of the monitoring time. Example: from 22:00 to 07:00.", t_language: "Language of the interface. Automatic = as set in the browser.", t_dev: "Choose for which device you set the values below. “All devices” applies to every device without its own settings.", t_devown: "On: this device gets its own values (e.g. a phone in the bedroom that should be more sensitive). Off: it uses the values of “All devices”.", t_sched: "Add periods with different thresholds. Example: 22:00–06:00 “8 dB louder than normal”, during the day the value above applies (e.g. 12 dB). The period marked green is active now.", help_toggle: "Show help", help_tip: "Show or hide explanations and ⓘ hints", f_mic_port: "Input (jack)", h_mic_port: "Usually choose “Microphone”.", t_mic_port: "Some sound cards have several inputs (microphone, line). If you only get noise, choose “Microphone” here and check that the mic is in the pink jack.", port_auto: "Automatic", port_na: "nothing plugged in",     hum: "Hum filter", hum_off: "off", hum_50: "50 Hz (Europe)", hum_60: "60 Hz (USA)", hum_na: "Hum filter not available – rebuild the add-on.", t_hum: "Removes mains hum (50 Hz and harmonics), common with USB sound cards. The filters are very narrow: knocks and voices are kept. Affects display, detection and stored measurements.", m_view: "View (chart + playback)",    ev_nograph: "No chart stored for this event (recorded before version 1.12).", ev_gone: "Event not found (deleted?).", ev_hint: "Click into the chart = playback from that point. Drag sideways = mark a range and name it. Long-press, right-click or key N = note at this position. Red = disturbance, light red = pre- and post-roll, before and after that the context. At the bottom the AI markers, at the top the named ranges.", refs_title: "Custom reference lines", refs_label: "Name, e.g. TV", refs_add: "Line", refs_add_now: "Line at current level", refs_add_now_tip: "Adds a line at the level this device measures right now (e.g. while the TV is on).", refs_now_label: "Measured", refs_nolevel: "No level for this device right now.", t_refs: "Your own horizontal lines in this device's chart, e.g. “TV −52 dB” or “loud knock −35 dB”. You see at a glance how loud something is in comparison. The lines do not change detection.",    listen_load: "loading …",     
